import
	../../html

intro-parallel html[]()
	- section "Simple parallelism",
		- p quote
			The simplest type of parallelism is a parallel {std-function "keen/parallel", "for-loop"}.
			{br}
			This returns results in the same order as the inputs.

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/parallel

			main void()
				inputs nat[] = 1, 2, 3
				results nat[] = for x in parallel inputs
					x * 10
				log results

		- aside-small quote
			JavaScript is not truly parallel,
			so these examples will work but won't get any performance benefit.

	- section "Mutability",
		- p quote
			The parallel {for_} loop takes a {shared_} lambda as its argument.
			{br}
			That means you can't access {mut_} values in the body of the loop.

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/parallel

			main void()
				inputs nat[] = 1, 2, 3
				res nat mut[] = ()
				for x in parallel inputs
					res ~= x # Not allowed
				log res

		- p quote
			You can use a {shared_} type though:

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/col/shared-array
				keen/parallel

			main void()
				inputs nat[] = 1, 2, 3
				res nat shared[] = ()
				for x in parallel inputs
					res ~= x
				log res.move-to::nat[]

	- section "Shared collections",
		- p quote
			A {t}{sp}{shared-array} is written as {t}{type " shared[]"}.
			{br}
			This supports a subset of the functions available for {mut-array}s.
		- p quote
			Like all shared collections, it does not define {function "iterate"}.
			Access to a {shared_} collection needs to quickly do just one thing
			before releasing the collection for other users,
			so it can't do bulk operations that access every element.
		- p quote
			You can, however, use {fn-move-to} to take the entire collection contents out to a new collection,
			leaving the original {shared-array} empty.

		- p quote
			There is also {key-value}{sp}{shared-map},
			written as {value}{sp}{shared_}{lbracket}{key}{rbracket}.
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/col/shared-map
				keen/parallel

			main void()
				inputs nat[] = 1, 2, 3
				res nat shared[nat] = ()
				for x in parallel inputs
					res[x] := x * 10
				log res.move-to::nat[nat]

	- section "Shared lambdas and exclusions",
		- p quote
			A {shared_} expression makes any lambda {shared_} even if it accesses mutable variables.
			{br}
			To do so, just prefix the lambda with the {shared_} keyword.

		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/parallel

			main void()
				accum mut nat = 0
				f void shared(x nat) = shared of x
					accum +:= x
				for x in parallel 0 .. 100
					f[x]
				log accum

		- aside-small quote
			Since there is a lock on {var-f}, there is no genuine parallelism in the above example,
			just 100 threads trying to acquire the same lock.

		- p quote
			This works by using an {em "exclusion"}.
			All Keen code runs in some exclusion; {fn-main} starts with a default one.
			{br}
			The {em "exclusion"} is a lock shared by all {mut_} variables in the same exclusion.
			So it's coarse-grained parallelism.
			{br}
			Each new thread launched by a parallel {for_} loop gets a new exclusion.
		- p quote
			Exclusions ensure that {mut_} types are safe to use.
			{br}
			When you use {shared_} on a lambda, it creates a lambda that locks the exclusion before it can be entered.
			That way the lambda can have only one caller at a time,
			meaining {variable "accum"} will only be accessed by one thread at a time.

		- p "Below, {var-f} and {var-g} will never run at the same time, because they share the same exclusion."
		- keen-runnable quote
			import
				keen/parallel

			main void()
				accum mut nat = 0
				f void shared(x nat) = shared of x
					accum +:= x
				g void shared(x nat) = shared of x
					accum -:= x
				for x in parallel 0 .. 100
					f[x]
					g[x]
				log accum

	- section "Safe parallelism",
		- p quote
			When a safe function returns, any threads that it launched should have been waited on.
			{br}
			A parallel {for_} loop satisfies this.
			{br}
			When calling Java and JS functions, don't mark the call as {trusted_} unless it satisfies this property.
